{
 "id": "2020-fumagalli-planting",
 "name": "Planting Along Rural Greenways: Cycling Videos (Fumagalli et al. 2020)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Fumagalli, N., Maccarini, M., Rovelli, R., Berto, R., & Senes, G. (2020). An Exploratory Study of Users' Preference for Different Planting Combinations along Rural Greenways. Sustainability 12:2120. https://doi.org/10.3390/su12052120\n\nWhich planting makes a rural cycle path most restorative? Each participant is randomly shown ONE of four rendered videos of a ride along a straight greenway in the Po Valley farmland: (1) no designed planting, (2) a row of columnar poplars on both sides, (3) native shrubs on the left and trees on the right, (4) shrubs on the left and perennials on the right. With the video kept above the questions for replay, they rate the 11 PRS-11 items plus preference and familiarity (0 = not at all, 6 = rather much, 10 = completely) and 13 physical–aesthetic attributes (0 = not at all … 5 = a lot), then answer socio-demographic and cycling questions. Original result (297 participants): scenario 3 was the most restorative (PRS-11 6.27) and most preferred (6.69), the unplanted greenway the least (5.70; 5.80).\n\nVideos: the paper's Videos S1–S4 (CC BY 4.0; SketchUp + Thea Render animations, not AI-generated), re-encoded to 854 px H.264 without sound.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the published supplementary videos are 20-second extracts; the original participants watched 60 seconds; (2) the PRS-11 English wording follows Pasini et al. as printed in Kim et al. (2022, IJERPH 19:10231), since the study itself was in Italian; (3) the three compatibility items from the full PRS are not printed and are left out; the preference and familiarity items and the attribute wording are short versions written for this template; (4) the answer options of the socio-demographic and cycling questions are not reported and were written for this template.",
 "author": "Fumagalli, Maccarini, Rovelli, Berto & Senes",
